WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.013
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.313 --> 00:00:08.746
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.046 --> 00:00:14.671
Unit seven: Formal Logic. Today you will construct and test arguments with conditional clauses.

4
00:00:14.971 --> 00:00:17.774
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:21.966
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.817 --> 00:00:23.667
Premise.

7
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:28.726
Premise means a statement assumed to be true from which a conclusion is drawn.

8
00:00:29.317 --> 00:00:31.804
The argument rests on a questionable premise.

9
00:00:32.417 --> 00:00:35.382
Your turn. Say: Premise.

10
00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:39.095
Premise.

11
00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:40.932
Syllogism.

12
00:00:41.917 --> 00:00:46.319
Syllogism means a form of reasoning with two premises and a conclusion.

13
00:00:46.917 --> 00:00:50.669
Aristotle formalised the syllogism over two thousand years ago.

14
00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:53.460
Your turn. Say: Syllogism.

15
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.185
Syllogism.

16
00:00:57.950 --> 00:00:58.928
Validity.

17
00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:04.238
Validity means the property of an argument whose conclusion follows necessarily.

18
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:07.221
Validity concerns form, not truth.

19
00:01:07.817 --> 00:01:10.562
Your turn. Say: Validity.

20
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.355
Validity.

21
00:01:15.117 --> 00:01:16.071
Fallacy.

22
00:01:17.083 --> 00:01:20.917
Fallacy means an error in reasoning that renders an argument unsound.

23
00:01:21.517 --> 00:01:23.969
Circular reasoning is a common fallacy.

24
00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:27.305
Your turn. Say: Fallacy.

25
00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:31.284
Fallacy.

26
00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:33.144
Deduction.

27
00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:38.274
Deduction means reasoning from general principles to a specific conclusion.

28
00:01:38.883 --> 00:01:41.301
Mathematical proof relies on deduction.

29
00:01:41.917 --> 00:01:44.592
Your turn. Say: Deduction.

30
00:01:47.433 --> 00:01:48.400
Deduction.

31
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:50.221
Induction.

32
00:01:51.217 --> 00:01:55.283
Induction means reasoning from specific observations to a general rule.

33
00:01:55.883 --> 00:01:58.301
Induction can never guarantee certainty.

34
00:01:58.917 --> 00:02:01.569
Your turn. Say: Induction.

35
00:02:04.433 --> 00:02:05.481
Induction.

36
00:02:06.217 --> 00:02:07.380
Inference.

37
00:02:08.383 --> 00:02:12.403
Inference means a conclusion reached on the basis of evidence and reasoning.

38
00:02:13.017 --> 00:02:15.457
The inference was plausible but untested.

39
00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.447
Your turn. Say: Inference.

40
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.394
Inference.

41
00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:24.116
Soundness.

42
00:02:25.117 --> 00:02:29.194
Soundness means the quality of a valid argument whose premises are all true.

43
00:02:29.783 --> 00:02:33.048
Soundness requires both valid form and true premises.

44
00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:36.070
Your turn. Say: Soundness.

45
00:02:38.933 --> 00:02:40.143
Soundness.

46
00:02:40.933 --> 00:02:44.468
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.317 --> 00:02:52.781
Visal, is the following argument valid? All fish can fly. A shark is a fish. Therefore, a shark can fly.

48
00:02:53.883 --> 00:03:01.136
Surprisingly, yes. If the premises are true, then the conclusion must follow. The form is perfectly valid.

49
00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.029
But sharks can't fly!

50
00:03:05.117 --> 00:03:11.867
Exactly. The argument is valid but not sound, because the first premise is false.

51
00:03:12.983 --> 00:03:15.354
So how would you test an argument in an essay?

52
00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:25.964
I ask two questions: if every premise were true, would the conclusion be unavoidable? And are the premises actually true?

53
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:28.507
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:32.592
Grammar focus: Conditional clauses (if and then).

55
00:03:32.892 --> 00:03:34.218
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:34.917 --> 00:03:38.426
If the premises are true, then the conclusion must follow.

57
00:03:39.317 --> 00:03:42.767
If one premise is false, then the argument cannot be sound.

58
00:03:43.683 --> 00:03:47.564
If the conclusion were false, then at least one premise would have to be false.

59
00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:57.166
In logic, 'if … then …' expresses necessity. Use 'must' for a certain conclusion, 'cannot' for an impossible one, and 'were … would' for hypothetical testing.

60
00:03:57.917 --> 00:04:02.984
Be careful! Don't say: If all premises are true, the conclusion must follows.

61
00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:08.883
Say: If all premises are true, the conclusion must follow.

62
00:04:09.183 --> 00:04:11.171
After 'must', use the base verb.

63
00:04:11.967 --> 00:04:14.665
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:15.467 --> 00:04:17.362
Valid Is Not the Same as True.

65
00:04:17.662 --> 00:04:22.099
Formal logic studies the structure of arguments rather than the truth of their content.

66
00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:27.770
An argument is valid when, if the premises are true, then the conclusion must follow.

67
00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:34.806
Consequently, an argument can be perfectly valid and yet reach an absurd conclusion from a false premise.

68
00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:41.457
Soundness is therefore stricter: it requires both valid form and premises that are actually true.

69
00:04:42.467 --> 00:04:47.705
Whereas deduction can guarantee its conclusions, induction can only make them more or less probable.

70
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.046
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:53.883 --> 00:04:57.032
If the premises are true, then the conclusion blank follow.

72
00:04:57.332 --> 00:05:00.521
A, must. B, should to. C, has.

73
00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:05.555
The answer is A: must.

74
00:05:06.717 --> 00:05:12.068
'All fish fly. Sharks are fish. So sharks fly.' This argument is blank.

75
00:05:12.368 --> 00:05:17.623
A, sound but invalid. B, valid but unsound. C, valid and sound.

76
00:05:21.167 --> 00:05:23.967
The answer is B: valid but unsound.

77
00:05:25.117 --> 00:05:28.045
Reasoning from observations to a general rule is blank.

78
00:05:28.345 --> 00:05:32.103
A, deduction. B, induction. C, syllogism.

79
00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:37.631
The answer is B: induction.

80
00:05:38.783 --> 00:05:42.617
If every premise blank true, would the conclusion be unavoidable?

81
00:05:42.917 --> 00:05:46.176
A, were. B, is being. C, be.

82
00:05:49.733 --> 00:05:51.222
The answer is A: were.

83
00:05:52.383 --> 00:05:55.567
According to the reading, what can induction NOT do?

84
00:05:55.867 --> 00:06:01.610
A, guarantee its conclusions. B, use observations. C, suggest probabilities.

85
00:06:05.167 --> 00:06:08.350
The answer is A: guarantee its conclusions.

86
00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:12.544
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:13.383 --> 00:06:16.497
What is the difference between a valid argument and a sound one?

88
00:06:33.283 --> 00:06:34.830
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:35.130 --> 00:07:05.097
A valid argument is one in which, if the premises are true, then the conclusion must follow. A sound argument goes one step further, because its premises really are true. For example, 'all cats can swim, Tom is a cat, so Tom can swim' is valid, but it is not sound. Some students confuse validity with truth, which is understandable. However, in logic, validity only describes the structure of reasoning.

90
00:07:05.397 --> 00:07:06.967
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:07.883 --> 00:07:12.579
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and conditional clauses (if and then).

92
00:07:12.879 --> 00:07:17.447
Today's value is rigor. Check every step carefully before you trust a conclusion.

93
00:07:17.747 --> 00:07:20.074
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

94
00:07:20.374 --> 00:07:23.154
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

